going from one football talk to another  football talk  we have  someone who  well didn't really know about football  but she won a whole football data  hackathon which is already impressive  enough  but perhaps more impressive is that she  is a jeep wrangler owner  and a thrift store power shopper  so  please everyone welcome asama  oh man  hi everyone i hope you can hear me uh  it's a pleasure to be here obviously i  wish i could see you all in person but  it is what it is um so i'm here to talk  about  my team and i's process through the 2021  nfl big data bowl  and so yeah let's get into it  so to paint the picture a little bit um  you know  the extent of my football knowledge was  really that it was a game comprised of  very large adult men who bulldoze one  another  for four quarters and the point is to  score a touchdown  or prevent them  and then there's things like fourth  downs which are hysterical because  people get really mad about their team's  decisions  at that point so that was really  the extent of it and being in the middle  of a pandemic  having really nothing to do with my free  time i'm like okay yeah let's let's get  into this american terrorist tradition  with no knowledge of it i'm like it's  it's gonna be fine i'll look at the data  do some eda i'll be able to scrap  something up together  um  i was pleasantly surprised that one  cannot in fact do that  uh  first of all the size is quite  overwhelming all the different events  as well  apologies for the blurry picture but i  think you can gain an understanding of  just how intense this was  for me  and i quickly realized that i needed to  get a team together hopefully with  people  that have a little bit more football  knowledge than i did  and then being who i am you know i was  hoping this was going to be a cute  little quarantine hobby  but being who i am in aries i'm like you  know what no no i want to win this thing  and given the theme of this con of this  hackathon it was  the topic was defense  and you know anyone who works in sports  will tell you defense is famously hard  to quantify  so i knew that with my team it just  wasn't going to be enough we needed to  maybe talk to a subject matter expert uh  maybe a coach so i started digging into  twitter and just seeing if there were  any active  nfl coaches  um and i fell upon the wonderful the one  and only mr wade phillips a championship  winning nfl coach who also was a  defensive coach  so once again me being who i am i  decided to cold dm him on a random  november wednesday night  um i was like hey i'm awesome i'm  participating in this thing i would love  like 30 minutes your time um  yeah and he was like sure  so  we we met him we got some really good  pointers on where to take our analysis  and then the work began  so in terms of our setup  this will be very familiar to all of us  who have continued to work during this  pandemic we were all our programmers so  we made sure to get up and running with  the latest version of r and use the pack  package  to  get all the packages we needed and to  upgrade them  github of course for collaboration that  was a consideration from the get-go  given that we were all in different  geographical areas and collaboration was  going to be of the essence  zoom for regular  check-ins and deep dives  and slack for quick communications  and code troubleshooting  so once we got our our setup you know  the the first and main consideration was  the data  so  i knew going in that this was going to  be pretty big data you know  on the on the kaggle page they had said  that this is an entire season's worth of  data and not only that it's tracking  data so that has the  x and y locations of every player  and the ball  10 times per second so we're getting  real real granular here and i decided to  just  bind all those 17 weeks worth of data  and it came to be about three gigabytes  and i'm like well because we're going to  be working with github how do i get this  data up  i had never pushed anything that big on  to github also i come from healthcare so  that's like a big no-no to ever push  data onto there  but i'm like you know what let me just  try and github had something to say and  the answer was hell no because our limit  is a hundred megabytes  um so i'm like  well okay uh i guess it's time to get  into some compression let's see what's  out there so i did some very  unscientific experimentation  with rda rds fst parque tsv  just things i've like never even heard  of and turns out that parquet or parquet  has overall the best compression  and the fastest read in time so shout  out to the arrow developers who may be  in attendance  you guys are the bomb um but then it  still wasn't enough i got it down to  about 200 ish megabytes but that's still  double  github's free size limit of course  there's get large file storage  but me being me and a goodwill power  shopper i hate spending money needlessly  so i decided to continue being  stubborn and try to find alternative  ways to get my data up on there and  turns out because the rstats community  literally thinks of everything  uh the r open side  developers have created the piggyback  package which is a workaround to get  data files up to two gigabytes per file  onto your github repo and the way it  works is that  these files are not handled by git in  any way  they just get on there by being tagged  as a github release  and once it's up there you can read it  in pretty easily with just like one line  of code  so that was that the data was taken care  of it's now in our github repo so it was  time to move on to the next stage of our  project  which is eda and future engineering and  we've gone over those with the  presentations um  so far caitlyn did a fantastic job just  laying out what eda  is and and how useful it is  but  um i would like to just take a moment to  talk about how tidyverse and other  packages have really  really been tremendously useful for us  in wrangling this  very large and quite complex spatial  temporal data set  many of these are  very familiar we're very familiar with  many of these but just a quick shout out  to the tibble package which goes a bit  underrated because it's well integrated  in literally every single one of these  but you know tibbles are a fantastic way  to just quickly inspect  your your outputs and  um they're also quote-unquote like they  complain more so if there's errors  it will tell you about it pretty early  on and that was important because our  project at the end ended up being  thousands of lines of code long with  certain scripts sourcing other scripts  so you know tibbles were really useful  in that error alerting functionality  because it helped us avoid just error  debugging nightmares  per special shout out to purr not  because i'm just a cat person but really  per  did a lot of work in helping us be  efficient  in applying functions over vectors and  lists it's cousin fur which helped us  gain even more speed efficiencies  skimar from the our open side community  really useful in getting quick summary  statistics shout out to janitor for  cleaning and quick frequency tables  lubridae  the greatest of all time  and this package memoize or momoys or  however you say it but this is a package  we first found out about through the  advanced r book  and  what it does is it caches function calls  so what that means is if it has  previously seen a set of inputs it will  return the previously computed output so  that's a mouthful just to say that if  you have very expensive functions  then certainly look into this package  because it will help make everything  faster so shout out to the memoirs  package and the advanced our book for  opening our eyes to many ways in which  we can make our code run faster and more  efficiently  so after the eda and the feature  engineering stage we got into the  modeling  we decided to go with xg boost and bart  bart standing for bayesian additive  regression trees these are  models are very popular in sports and  many other industries because they do  really well  with large data sets they do well with  data sets containing heterogeneous  features  they handle interaction effects with  minimal involvement from the analyst and  they have shown to have  superior predictive performance  we also use tidy models but more for the  data pre-processing steps  and this was a choice because we were in  a time pinch and we had experience  fitting models with xg boost and bart  but i think if we were to do it again i  think we would want a tidy model's  pipeline from end to end  just because tidy models is streamlined  there's  less guesswork involved and once you  invest that time up front to get  acquainted with the grammar  then it pays off  in terms of iterations and modeling is  inherently an inter iterative process  it also  tidy models as the name suggests is  part of the tidy verse so you get also  all the the benefits of functions and  pipe ability and tibbles  and actually just a side anecdote  it was quite difficult sometimes  wrangling the outputs of these  models for further manipulation like  data visualizations or feeding it into  other  packages for model interpretability but  if we had  done our entire  process with tidy models all the tidy  model outputs are tibbles and tibbles  are very malleable so  definitely a little lesson learned there  and then once our modeling was done it  was time to start thinking about  visualization and presentation now  visualization was always a part of all  the other steps i i talked about but for  this competition  specifically it really stressed the  quality of the submission so we knew we  had to step up our game there  of course given that our entire pipeline  was  tidy verse we made heavy use of gg plot  2 but really the magic i think was when  we paired it with gg animate which  allows you to animate your graphs so  it's definitely a powerful technique it  can also be misused i know certainly  that when i get exposed to like a new  technique i like want to use it on  everything ever and it's  with animation it can be distracting and  also not entirely justified so we made  sure to really convince ourselves that  this was a value add to our graphics and  given the context of the data that we're  working on which is spatial temporal  then it made a lot of sense to make use  of animation  for the purposes of really  giving the  audience time to digest what is going on  in a way that is not  overwhelming given how busy these graphs  are um we packed a lot in here uh given  just the restrictions of the competition  i think they kept it at like five  visualizations so because we packed a  lot in here animation uh was definitely  very justified here  another key player in our visualization  roster was the gt package which i  believe stands for grammar of tables so  because our pipeline once again entirely  tidy verse this was just able to plug  right in  to to our work because the typical gt  workflow starts with a table that you  can then  pipe other  arguments to to iteratively add more  layers to your table so we were able to  very quickly once we got acquainted with  the grammar  very quickly generate you know  professional clean looking tables so in  this one you can see a heat map  component added onto it you can also see  the ability to  bold certain things or call attention to  certain rows  you can even add footnotes you could  even add pictures  um from in this case we added pictures  of teams so this was really a huge value  add in terms of our submission  last but not least we made use of shiny  and this was a decision  based upon the fact that i think caitlyn  said this in her presentation it's  important to get buy-in uh from people  and one of the first things that  your audience will want to see is a peek  into your model and your results so we  made the decision to create a shiny app  so that  the audience could for themselves slice  and dice the data and inspect our  results  and  the way  the way this came together was i mean  less than an hour we got this together  and we made use also  of packages that are built on html  widgets so on the left hand side this is  a plotly graph with  crosstalk and on the  right-hand side is a dt table so full  fully interactive table and the magic  here is with crosstalk i think because  you can choose for example to select  just three points from the graph and  when you do it will automatically filter  down that data table so  that was also a really nice  functionality that i think our audience  appreciated  so what we learned and learned is in  quotation marks here because we all work  in data science in a professional  capacity so we were all in  on on the tools that our studio offers  however i think there's like  not a replacement for you know truly tr  stress testing these tools on you know a  timed competition with um really complex  data so  tidy verse and tidy models  really they we saw that it enforced good  methodology and statistical practice and  i think if we were to do it again we  would definitely go with the tidy models  approach just because it takes out a lot  of guesswork and it comes with  really sensible defaults  presentation matters a lot i i really  think that our team didn't do anything  groundbreaking we didn't reinvent the  wheel but i think our  our attention to detail and you know  going the extra step with creating a  shiny app uh really helped um gain a  competitive edge  advanced r book taught us a lot of  efficiency tips and deepened our  knowledge of how certain packages work  particularly per and expose us to new  packages like lemoys so  um  i we  we went through this book together  through r for ds book club so highly  recommend if if you do want to deepen  your knowledge while having fun to join  one of those  github unfortunately is a necessary evil  it was a pain in the ass for for  multiple times but i think we couldn't  have done it without  github  we would like for next time to maybe  create a helper package or two just  because i kind of hinted at this but we  ended up  sourcing a lot of scripts multiple times  and reusing functions multiple times and  they say when you are doing that you  might as well just package it  and  make your life a little more streamlined  and easier  and i think one thing also is  we wanted to make sure that our  presentation  everything that was public facing was as  simple as possible without really  sacrificing complexity  so we really wanted to tailor the  submission to both enthusiasts hobbyists  but also you know the professional  analysts that were that were also  judging the submission  and last but not least designate a  project manager this was really helpful  because this is all something that we  did for fun we weren't getting paid to  do it you know preferably pick someone  that isn't afraid to shoot their shots  into cold dm celebrities online so  um definitely designate a sort of  decision maker in that in that regard  but really i saved the most important  lesson for last if you recall this is  the tweet my first tweet in the  presentation where i was like i don't  understand what any of these events mean  and someone from the r stats community  came to my help and created a cheat  sheet and i think this is just a  tremendous testament to the solidarity  that exists in our community  and maybe the real treasure and the real  lesson is the friends we made along the  way  so with that being said  um i want to thank my teammates um  my role models really honestly  marshall sydney tony and we did it we  won and we'll be back so if you are  participating we'll see you then  thank you  thank you very much for that talk  so that is

